---
import { PATHS, SITE } from "../data/site";
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Person",
  name: SITE.name,
  url: SITE.url,
  email: SITE.email,
  jobTitle: SITE.role,
  worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE.employer },
  sameAs: [SITE.youtube, SITE.github, SITE.linkedin],
};

const stack = [
  { label: "Languages", value: "Java" },
  { label: "Backend", value: "Spring Boot · Spring MVC · Spring Data JPA · Hibernate" },
  { label: "Data", value: "PostgreSQL · Oracle · Redis" },
  { label: "Messaging & Systems", value: "RabbitMQ · REST · Event-driven architecture · Distributed systems" },
  { label: "Infrastructure", value: "Docker · Kubernetes · Linux · Maven · Git" },
] as const;
---

<PageLayout
  title="About"
  description="Backend Engineer at Jio Platforms in Mumbai. Teaching Computer Science in public, from fundamentals to the systems built on top of them."
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />

  <section class="wrap page-head">
    <p class="section-kicker">About</p>
    <h1 class="display mt-5 max-w-2xl">Building systems. Understanding how they work. Teaching what I learn.</h1>
    <div class="rule-cyan mt-5"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap pb-16 md:pb-20">
    <div class="max-w-[46rem] min-w-0 space-y-6 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-muted">
    <p class="text-ink">
      I’m {SITE.name}, a {SITE.role} at {SITE.employer} in Mumbai, working across Java, Spring Boot, distributed systems, cloud infrastructure, and event-driven architecture.
    </p>
    <p>
      I spend my working days building backend systems that have to work under real constraints: quotas, queues, failures, replication, provisioning, and recovery.
    </p>
    <p>
      And outside work, I’m doing something slightly different: I’m teaching Computer Science in public.
    </p>

    <h2 class="title pt-6 text-ink">What I Do</h2>
    <p>
      As a Backend Engineer, I work on infrastructure and services that power cloud and AI workloads at {SITE.employer}.
    </p>
    <p>
      My work spans Java 17, Spring Boot, PostgreSQL, Oracle, Redis, RabbitMQ, REST APIs, Docker, Kubernetes, and asynchronous processing.
    </p>
    <p>I’ve worked on systems for:</p>
    <p class="font-mono text-sm leading-7 text-ink">
      Quota validation → Order processing → Async workflows → Observability → Disaster recovery → Data synchronization
    </p>
    <p>The interesting part isn’t just writing the code.</p>
    <p>
      It’s understanding what happens when the system meets scale, concurrency, failure, and real-world constraints.
    </p>

    <h2 class="title pt-6 text-ink">Why Computer Science?</h2>
    <p>Because frameworks change.</p>
    <p>Languages evolve.</p>
    <p>Tools come and go.</p>
    <p class="text-ink">But the fundamentals stay.</p>
    <p>
      Understanding memory, data structures, operating systems, networks, databases, concurrency, and distributed systems gives you a mental model that lasts far beyond a specific technology stack.
    </p>
    <p>That’s what I’m interested in.</p>
    <p>Not just how to use a tool. How the system behind it actually works.</p>

    <h2 class="title pt-6 text-ink">Teaching Computer Science in Public</h2>
    <p>I’m currently building a fundamentals-first Computer Science course in public.</p>
    <p>The approach is simple:</p>
    <p class="font-mono text-sm leading-7 text-ink">
      Start with the concept.
      <br />
      Understand it from first principles.
      <br />
      Solve problems that test the mental model.
      <br />
      Then build a system that puts the idea into code.
    </p>
    <p>
      The course starts with Memory &amp; Low-Level Concepts and will move through the foundations of modern software engineering, including data structures, algorithms, operating systems, computer networks, databases, distributed systems, backend engineering, security, cloud, and machine learning.
    </p>
    <p>The goal isn’t to cover more topics. It’s to understand the connections between them.</p>
    <p>
      <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={PATHS.course}>Explore the course →</a>
    </p>

    <h2 class="title pt-6 text-ink">A Little Beyond the Backend</h2>
    <p>
      Before backend engineering became my focus, I built Kavach, an Android SOS application using Java, XML, and Firebase.
    </p>
    <p>
      It went on to win a Gold Medal at Anveshana 2023 and was shortlisted for the Government of India Tech Innovation Awards.
    </p>
    <p>It was an early lesson in what I still enjoy today:</p>
    <p class="text-ink">Take an idea. Build it. Test it in the real world. Learn from what breaks.</p>

    <h2 class="title pt-6 text-ink">The Stack</h2>
    <dl class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-[11rem_minmax(0,1fr)]">
      {
        stack.map((row) => (
          <>
            <dt class="caption text-faint">{row.label}</dt>
            <dd class="text-ink">{row.value}</dd>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <h2 class="title pt-6 text-ink">What I’m Curious About</h2>
    <p class="text-ink">How does it work underneath?</p>
    <p>
      That question has taken me from writing code to understanding systems, and from learning Computer Science to teaching it.
    </p>
    <p>There’s always another abstraction to peel back.</p>
    <p>So I keep going one layer deeper.</p>

    <h2 class="title pt-6 text-ink">Currently</h2>
    <p class="text-ink">Filming Season 1: Memory &amp; Low-Level</p>
    <p>Learning. Building. Teaching in public.</p>
    <p class="font-mono text-sm">
      <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={SITE.youtube} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube</a>
      {" · "}
      <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={SITE.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>
      {" · "}
      <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={SITE.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a>
    </p>
    <p class="pt-4 text-ink">{SITE.name}</p>
    <p>
      {SITE.role} · {SITE.educatorRole}
      <br />
      {SITE.location}
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="text-accent hover:underline break-all" href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>
    </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
